1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
Windows. Icons. Menus. A mouse.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,720
For most people, that is simply
what a computer looks like.

3
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:09,700
And most of them met it through Microsoft.

4
00:00:10,362 --> 00:00:15,202
But when the first Windows appeared,
in 1985, almost none of that was new.

5
00:00:15,642 --> 00:00:18,202
Other companies had already built it.

6
00:00:18,801 --> 00:00:20,861
So here is the real question.

7
00:00:20,941 --> 00:00:24,621
If Microsoft didn't invent
the graphical interface,

8
00:00:24,701 --> 00:00:27,701
how did Windows end up on
most of the world's desks?

9
00:00:28,499 --> 00:00:31,699
To answer that, we have to look
at everyone who got there first...

10
00:00:31,779 --> 00:00:33,699
and ask why they didn't win.

11
00:00:36,199 --> 00:00:40,619
In 1968, in San Francisco,
a researcher called Douglas

12
00:00:40,849 --> 00:00:44,579
Engelbart sat on a stage in
front of about a thousand people.

13
00:00:45,191 --> 00:00:47,071
For ninety minutes, he
showed them a computer

14
00:00:47,301 --> 00:00:51,771
controlled with a small
wooden box on wheels. A mouse.

15
00:00:52,457 --> 00:00:54,797
Text on his screen linked to other text.

16
00:00:54,877 --> 00:00:59,697
And a colleague thirty miles away was
editing the same document with him.

17
00:01:00,754 --> 00:01:02,814
Engelbart showed where computing could go.

18
00:01:02,894 --> 00:01:07,334
But it was a research system, on
one large, expensive computer.

19
00:01:08,034 --> 00:01:09,814
Nobody could buy it.

20
00:01:11,974 --> 00:01:14,994
Five years later, a few
people came much closer.

21
00:01:15,825 --> 00:01:18,765
At Xerox PARC, a research
centre in California,

22
00:01:18,905 --> 00:01:21,265
a team built a machine called the Alto.

23
00:01:22,225 --> 00:01:24,405
The first ones ran in 1973.

24
00:01:25,523 --> 00:01:29,463
The Alto had a screen where every single
dot could be controlled separately.

25
00:01:29,883 --> 00:01:31,843
So it could show different fonts.

26
00:01:31,923 --> 00:01:33,403
Pictures.

27
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:36,623
And text that looked the
way it would on paper.

28
00:01:37,337 --> 00:01:40,897
It had a mouse. It had
windows that overlapped.

29
00:01:41,477 --> 00:01:43,777
It had menus that popped
up where you clicked.

30
00:01:44,098 --> 00:01:45,918
And it wasn't a lab curiosity.

31
00:01:46,918 --> 00:01:49,398
Xerox built around two thousand of them.

32
00:01:49,938 --> 00:01:53,998
People used them every day, to write,
to draw, to send each other email.

33
00:01:54,458 --> 00:02:00,198
So by the mid-1970s, a large part of
the modern desktop already existed.

34
00:02:00,558 --> 00:02:02,818
A decade before Windows.

35
00:02:03,601 --> 00:02:07,181
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

36
00:02:07,481 --> 00:02:09,481
It really helps the channel.

37
00:02:10,321 --> 00:02:12,761
So why aren't we all
using Xerox computers?

38
00:02:13,838 --> 00:02:16,678
The Alto itself was
never sold as a product.

39
00:02:17,378 --> 00:02:20,098
But in 1981, Xerox did release a

40
00:02:20,328 --> 00:02:24,458
commercial machine built on
the same ideas. The Star.

41
00:02:25,202 --> 00:02:29,662
The Star had a desktop on the screen.
Documents. Folders. A printer.

42
00:02:29,892 --> 00:02:31,402
An in-tray for mail.

43
00:02:32,084 --> 00:02:36,244
The idea that your screen looks like
an office desk went on sale here.

44
00:02:36,367 --> 00:02:40,313
And each Star workstation cost
more than sixteen thousand dollars.

45
00:02:40,393 --> 00:02:43,013
That was more than many
people earned in a year.

46
00:02:43,873 --> 00:02:46,253
And Xerox sold it to
offices as part of a whole

47
00:02:46,483 --> 00:02:49,453
networked system, not
as a single computer.

48
00:02:50,134 --> 00:02:52,054
It was enormously influential.

49
00:02:52,834 --> 00:02:54,454
It was a commercial failure.

50
00:02:55,203 --> 00:02:57,303
Xerox had the ideas first.

51
00:02:57,823 --> 00:03:00,743
It didn't have a machine
ordinary people could afford.

52
00:03:03,563 --> 00:03:05,323
Which brings us to Apple.

53
00:03:06,112 --> 00:03:11,692
In 1979, Steve Jobs and a group of
Apple engineers visited Xerox PARC.

54
00:03:12,651 --> 00:03:14,591
Xerox let them in on a deal.

55
00:03:15,251 --> 00:03:18,611
It got to buy a stake in Apple
before the company went public.

56
00:03:19,298 --> 00:03:21,898
What they saw changed
what Apple was building.

57
00:03:22,538 --> 00:03:24,718
But Apple didn't simply copy it.

58
00:03:25,038 --> 00:03:30,058
It hired people from PARC, and its own
engineers spent years reworking the ideas.

59
00:03:30,875 --> 00:03:37,695
The first result was the Lisa, in 1983.
A graphical interface. A mouse. Windows.

60
00:03:37,925 --> 00:03:39,009
Menus.

61
00:03:39,089 --> 00:03:41,976
And a price of nearly
ten thousand dollars.

62
00:03:42,069 --> 00:03:43,609
The Lisa failed too.

63
00:03:44,265 --> 00:03:46,965
Then, in 1984, came the Macintosh.

64
00:03:47,902 --> 00:03:51,922
Smaller. Simpler. About two
and a half thousand dollars.

65
00:03:52,002 --> 00:03:57,042
And launched with one of the most famous
adverts ever shown during a Super Bowl.

66
00:03:57,623 --> 00:04:00,883
This was the graphical
interface as a consumer product.

67
00:04:01,345 --> 00:04:04,445
So if anyone was going to
win, surely it was Apple?

68
00:04:05,353 --> 00:04:10,273
There was one catch. If you wanted a
Macintosh, you had to buy it from Apple.

69
00:04:10,976 --> 00:04:13,916
Apple controlled both the
hardware and the software.

70
00:04:14,235 --> 00:04:16,635
That made the Mac polished and consistent.

71
00:04:17,555 --> 00:04:21,775
It also meant that the Mac could only
spread as fast as Apple could sell it.

72
00:04:22,469 --> 00:04:24,389
And to see why that mattered, we have to

73
00:04:24,619 --> 00:04:26,889
leave the graphical
interface for a moment...

74
00:04:27,149 --> 00:04:28,809
and go back to 1980.

75
00:04:30,333 --> 00:04:34,233
That year, IBM was building
its first personal computer.

76
00:04:34,933 --> 00:04:36,913
And it needed an operating system.

77
00:04:37,577 --> 00:04:40,237
It went to a small
company called Microsoft.

78
00:04:41,129 --> 00:04:42,749
Microsoft didn't have one.

79
00:04:42,829 --> 00:04:47,229
So it licensed an existing system
from another Seattle company,

80
00:04:47,309 --> 00:04:51,189
bought it outright the following
year, and adapted it for IBM.

81
00:04:52,150 --> 00:04:56,063
On IBM's machines, it was called PC DOS.

82
00:04:56,363 --> 00:04:59,503
And here is the detail
the whole story turns on.

83
00:05:00,065 --> 00:05:02,985
IBM's contract did not stop Microsoft from

84
00:05:03,215 --> 00:05:05,885
licensing the same system
to other manufacturers.

85
00:05:06,902 --> 00:05:10,622
Microsoft kept the right to sell
it to anyone else... as MS-DOS.

86
00:05:11,862 --> 00:05:16,642
At first, that didn't seem to matter
very much. Who else would need it?

87
00:05:17,787 --> 00:05:20,347
The IBM PC came out in 1981.

88
00:05:20,577 --> 00:05:25,387
And IBM had built it mostly
from parts anyone could buy.

89
00:05:26,013 --> 00:05:29,573
The one piece rivals couldn't
legally copy was a small program

90
00:05:29,653 --> 00:05:32,953
IBM had written for a chip
at the heart of the machine.

91
00:05:33,618 --> 00:05:37,518
In 1983, a company called
Compaq started selling a

92
00:05:37,748 --> 00:05:41,198
computer that ran the same
software as an IBM PC.

93
00:05:42,013 --> 00:05:44,593
Its engineers had recreated
that small program

94
00:05:44,823 --> 00:05:48,173
from scratch, without copying IBM's code.

95
00:05:48,786 --> 00:05:50,246
Others did the same.

96
00:05:50,946 --> 00:05:55,386
Within a few years, dozens of companies
were selling IBM-compatible PCs.

97
00:05:56,372 --> 00:06:01,112
They all competed with each other.
On price. On speed. On features.

98
00:06:01,489 --> 00:06:04,949
And almost all of them ran
Microsoft's operating system.

99
00:06:05,395 --> 00:06:07,715
So by the time Windows arrived, Microsoft

100
00:06:07,945 --> 00:06:11,115
already had something
Xerox and Apple didn't.

101
00:06:11,550 --> 00:06:13,370
It wasn't a better interface.

102
00:06:14,050 --> 00:06:18,490
It was a place on a huge number of
computers made by companies it didn't own.

103
00:06:21,340 --> 00:06:24,380
Windows 1.0 came out in 1985.

104
00:06:25,404 --> 00:06:27,664
And honestly... it wasn't very good.

105
00:06:28,359 --> 00:06:30,539
Its windows couldn't even overlap.

106
00:06:31,139 --> 00:06:33,579
They were laid out side
by side, like tiles.

107
00:06:34,151 --> 00:06:38,151
But it ran on top of DOS, on
the PCs people already had.

108
00:06:38,771 --> 00:06:40,711
You didn't need a new computer.

109
00:06:40,971 --> 00:06:43,671
You didn't need to throw
away your old programs.

110
00:06:44,577 --> 00:06:49,257
Windows 2.0, in 1987,
added overlapping windows.

111
00:06:50,006 --> 00:06:53,206
And in 1988, Apple sued Microsoft.

112
00:06:54,027 --> 00:06:57,347
Apple claimed Windows copied the
look and feel of the Macintosh.

113
00:06:57,983 --> 00:07:01,623
But in 1985, Apple had
signed an agreement that let

114
00:07:01,853 --> 00:07:04,983
Microsoft use parts of the
Macintosh's visual design.

115
00:07:05,759 --> 00:07:07,519
The court found that
most of what Apple was

116
00:07:07,749 --> 00:07:10,259
complaining about was
covered by that licence...

117
00:07:10,339 --> 00:07:12,539
or couldn't be protected at all.

118
00:07:13,066 --> 00:07:16,146
Nobody could own the idea
of a window on a screen.

119
00:07:16,810 --> 00:07:18,950
The case dragged on for six years.

120
00:07:19,630 --> 00:07:23,290
And while it did, the real contest
was being decided somewhere else.

121
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:28,800
In 1990, Microsoft released Windows 3.0.

122
00:07:30,467 --> 00:07:32,127
It was finally good enough.

123
00:07:32,207 --> 00:07:36,787
And it could run on the huge number
of PCs that were already out there.

124
00:07:37,327 --> 00:07:41,927
PC makers started installing it on
new computers before they were sold.

125
00:07:42,143 --> 00:07:45,043
And Microsoft made sure
that was the easy choice.

126
00:07:45,845 --> 00:07:49,045
Under some of its licences,
a manufacturer paid Microsoft

127
00:07:49,275 --> 00:07:52,545
for every computer it shipped
with a certain processor...

128
00:07:53,105 --> 00:07:56,525
whether that computer came with
Microsoft's software or not.

129
00:07:56,819 --> 00:07:59,939
So installing a rival
system meant paying twice.

130
00:08:00,734 --> 00:08:04,294
In 1994, the United States
government challenged

131
00:08:04,524 --> 00:08:07,854
those contracts, and
Microsoft agreed to stop.

132
00:08:08,225 --> 00:08:11,825
But by then, something much harder
to stop was already running.

133
00:08:12,438 --> 00:08:16,538
Software companies wrote programs
for the platform with the most users.

134
00:08:17,227 --> 00:08:19,967
Users bought the platform
with the most programs.

135
00:08:20,822 --> 00:08:24,302
Every new Windows user made
Windows more worth writing for.

136
00:08:24,522 --> 00:08:27,822
Every new program made
Windows more worth buying.

137
00:08:28,193 --> 00:08:32,433
And once that loop gets going, it is
very hard for anyone else to get in.

138
00:08:35,253 --> 00:08:39,273
In 1995, Microsoft released Windows 95.

139
00:08:40,235 --> 00:08:42,355
A Start button. A taskbar.

140
00:08:42,475 --> 00:08:43,975
Long file names.

141
00:08:44,327 --> 00:08:48,887
And a launch so loud that people queued
at midnight to buy an operating system.

142
00:08:49,543 --> 00:08:53,583
Microsoft said it sold seven million
copies in the first five weeks.

143
00:08:56,803 --> 00:09:00,823
So how did Windows win without
inventing the graphical interface?

144
00:09:02,235 --> 00:09:07,115
Not by being first. Engelbart
showed the way in 1968.

145
00:09:07,835 --> 00:09:11,475
Xerox made it work in the 1970s.

146
00:09:12,255 --> 00:09:14,995
Apple put it in people's homes in 1984.

147
00:09:15,684 --> 00:09:17,324
And not by being the best.

148
00:09:17,984 --> 00:09:20,664
For years, Windows was
clearly behind the Mac.

149
00:09:21,393 --> 00:09:24,153
Windows won because
Microsoft had spent a decade

150
00:09:24,383 --> 00:09:27,113
getting its software onto
other people's computers.

151
00:09:27,531 --> 00:09:29,811
When the graphical interface
finally became something

152
00:09:30,041 --> 00:09:33,491
everyone wanted, Microsoft
was already there.

153
00:09:34,049 --> 00:09:37,749
Nobody invented the graphical
interface in a single moment.

154
00:09:38,169 --> 00:09:41,909
It was built in steps, by many
people, over twenty years.

155
00:09:42,531 --> 00:09:45,171
Microsoft's contribution wasn't the idea.

156
00:09:45,811 --> 00:09:48,961
It was getting the idea onto
most of the desks on the planet.
